{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And you shall keep the Sabbath.</b> One must know why He returned to command the keeping of the Sabbath and to give a new reason: ״כִּי קֹדֶשׁ״. It appears that because He had already permitted the desecration of the Sabbath for a sick person, as we explained in the preceding verse, He was concerned lest a mistaken person err and think that regarding such a sick person the Sabbath becomes profane and may be desecrated even for details of healing that do not involve danger to life — therefore Scripture says <b>and you shall keep the Sabbath, for it is holy</b>, meaning that in its holiness it stands firm for all other details that do not involve danger to life." "It may further be explained based on what they said, their memory for a blessing (Menachot 64a): they assessed it for two fig-cakes, etc., and there were two stalks — one with two fig-cakes and one with three; they may cut only the stalk with two and not the one with three, end quote. Now, in terms of the act that a person performs, it is one and the same whether he cuts the one with two or the one with three; but in relation to the act done on the Sabbath, the cutting of two versus the cutting of three differs. And this is what the verse says: <b>and you shall keep the Sabbath</b>, meaning that no act of labor that is not needed for that sick person should be done on it; <b>for it is holy</b> — it suspends the matter upon the Sabbath, and if so, when they cut a stalk with three when he needs only two, the Sabbath is found to be profaned to the extent of the third, and this is a profanation of its holiness, for even in relation to this sick person who is in danger, the Sabbath stands in its holiness." "<b>Those who profane it shall surely be put to death.</b> It says <b>מְחַלְלֶיהָ</b> in the plural and concludes by saying it in the singular. This may be explained based on what we explained, that there is one who profanes in the manner mentioned — who was permitted to cut a stalk of two figs for a sick person yet cut one of three — and there is one who cut with no need at all, and both are called profaners (מְחַלְּלִים); but only one of them is liable to death — namely, the one who cut for the healing of an endangered patient but cut in the place of two, one of three. And since the essential act of labor was done with permission, he is not liable to the death penalty." "According to the plain sense of the verse, it further intends: <b>מְחַלְלֶיהָ</b> — whether one acts in the presence of witnesses and after warning, or whether one acts privately between himself and himself. One of them — namely, the one who acts in the presence of witnesses and was warned — is the one who is put to death; but the second is not put to death, but rather <b>וְנִכְרְתָה</b> etc. — in the judgment of El Elyon." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend based on their words, their memory for a blessing (Shabbat 106b): one sat at the opening and did not fill it, and the second sat and filled it — the second is liable. And this is what the verse says: <b>מְחַלְלֶיהָ</b>, meaning that since this labor was performed only by means of these two people — for without the first it would not have been trapped — it turns out that two profaned it. And this is what it says: <b>מְחַלְלֶיהָ מוֹת יוּמָת</b> — only one of them is liable." "It may further be explained based on what they said, of blessed memory (Beitzah 34a), and this is their language: one of them brings the wood, and one brings the spices, and one stirs the pot, and one brings the water. The Gemara concludes that if the one who brought the wood was last, he is liable and all the rest are exempt. And this is what the verse says — <b>those who profane it</b>: one of them is the one who is liable to death, and all the rest are exempt." "<b>He shall surely be put to death.</b> I have already explained in the portion of Mishpatim (Exodus 21:12) the reason for the doubling, for this man is a son of death before the heavens, and HaShem authorizes the lower beings to execute this son of death, and to withdraw the other son of death so that his judgment is not given over to creatures but to the heavens. And that is what He mentions nearby: <b>whoever does work on it shall be cut off</b>, etc." "<b>For whoever does.</b> Meaning, the reason I reminded you of the singular form in <b>those who profane it</b> — one should not think that the other profaner who is not subject to witnesses, etc., has no punishment of death at all, but is merely like one who transgresses a negative and positive commandment of the sanctity of Shabbat — it is not so; rather, <b>everyone who does work on it shall be cut off</b>, that soul, even without witnesses and warning. From this point on, what the verse minimized by using the singular form applies only to the matter of rendering judgment in an earthly court. And according to what I explained regarding the verse <b>those who profane it</b> — that what the verse minimized by using the singular form refers to one who cuts the tail-feather that has three sections for a sick person who needs only two, so that even if there were warning and witnesses he would be exempt — it was necessary to say <b>for whoever does</b>, etc., so that you should not infer from the implication of the minimization that applies to the one who acts intentionally without witnesses, as mentioned, that he bears no punishment; hence the verse says <b>for</b>, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:17.520719714Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}